#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124328 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124519 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a08e0a4-22c3-5139-848d-6f1027a61056" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Is in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "e5f16c73-7422-57db-9f63-d1e593b8c3be" :question/math-academy-id "e-5442" :question/problem "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ such that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{b}.$ In the affirmative case, find such a vector.\n\n![](images/59/5900ef8431b10423f3db69a6940b4d91f8834ab7f9f84f874408c5746b2af56f.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $\\mathbf{T},$ we have $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\textrm{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}),$ we need to check if the equation $T\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent. \n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T \\,|\\, \\mathbf{b}],$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 & -10 & 26 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -16 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -16 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has a zero row, and so the system is consistent. We have two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). So, $x_3$ is a free variable. \nNow, from the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n-8x_2-16x_3 = 8\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_2=-1-2x_3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n2x_1+4(-1-2x_3)+2x_3 = 6\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_1=5+3x_3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by \n![](images/0c/0cef947192a22907ea689c9cd0a37e7acd81391510e41182df130931384f8c98.svg)\n\nSetting $x_3=0,$ we have\n![](images/33/33b3800ded425b00142df2d1fe78687c30734466a9b96a4b3597164261c162de.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124329 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124520 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e1baaf3c-f907-59dc-b9f9-a476a2889e73" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rank of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "5b6f9536-7ac0-57d6-b5cd-d9ce4f4e2620" :question/math-academy-id "e-5443" :question/problem "If ![](images/16/162985353a97de2cf240f58e94ae35f336555cd4fa090f2ecaff974cf2d12528.svg) is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ then what is $\\text{rank}(\\mathbf{T})?$" :question/worked-solution "In general, $\\text{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(\\mathbf{T})) = \\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 5 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 7 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{7}{4}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $2$ pivot columns, we have that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{rank}(T)=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124356 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124547 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4230d6ad-86c4-565e-8c5f-ab08179fc26f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Component of a Vector in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "372bf9eb-6665-5593-8aab-0fa31f5483d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-5619" :question/problem "Let ![](images/62/6226c3da91bd730306b56dd8fdc5fc9e1bccdcf65787c60d9d36a6b5ab1eb7e6.svg) be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector ![](images/9f/9f0c5eb4d90e62e01fe747f6fef8865e189e24439028537c79fdb0754bf224bb.svg) belongs to $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ belongs to $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ if there exists a vector ![](images/05/051bbfe8ee4c670fb4f23f639a75117085167e5e9363850b014961aa0b0363ba.svg) such that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{v}.$ \n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ -x+y \\\\ 2y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ k \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y=4 \\\\ -x+y=k \\\\ 2y=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $y=4,$ which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =4 \\\\ x+4 & =4 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0$ and $y=4$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+y & =k \\\\ 0+4 & =k \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{v}\\in\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ when $k=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272018 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124547 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4230d6ad-86c4-565e-8c5f-ab08179fc26f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Component of a Vector in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05afaaf0-89b1-5ce4-a384-1ae117b7f130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269320} {:answer/id #uuid "59d30bdf-5a36-54e4-8d9f-fda76a50bc4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270069} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4ada61-71c1-5abf-ad83-6da49b958a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270099} {:answer/id #uuid "7dfa1c82-7cc4-5c63-97ec-e7401f26d2cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270372} {:answer/id #uuid "fff491a4-876b-587a-8f17-f9d78c26ea1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fff491a4-876b-587a-8f17-f9d78c26ea1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271505} :answer-field/id #uuid "d23e8dfa-25d2-5a67-a900-3a5ab3411de6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "701a2c58-508c-5dda-9dee-75bbafc3bc3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140068" :question/problem "Let $T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$  belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$\n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot u & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2x-4y \\\\ x-3y \\\\ -x+4y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-4y=2 \\\\ x-3y=7 \\\\ -x+4y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=7+3y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4y & =2 \\\\ 2(7+3y)-4y & =2 \\\\ 2y & =-12 \\\\ y & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7+3y \\\\  & =7+3(-6) \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-11$ and $y=-6$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+4y & =k \\\\ -(-11)+4(-6) & =k \\\\ k & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=-13.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388646 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124519 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a08e0a4-22c3-5139-848d-6f1027a61056" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Is in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c1e333-e2c9-50b6-9471-65c86d9444fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388648} {:answer/id #uuid "8a795708-205b-56df-952f-53a06c207e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388649} {:answer/id #uuid "d5eedfa4-c0b4-5d38-8177-e7705183a67d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388650} {:answer/id #uuid "a16d9999-21ae-5936-b29b-4f1c0c5a27da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388651} {:answer/id #uuid "c3352a14-69cd-554c-848c-0087c58d946a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3352a14-69cd-554c-848c-0087c58d946a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388652} :answer-field/id #uuid "910d4aaa-e4db-5868-9808-87887dacb3b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4027f898-11dc-5b09-bb9b-58bf2f6ee48d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140073" :question/problem "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{3}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of $2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b],$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 9 & 17\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 9 & 17\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -6 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. \nNow, from the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n-{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=1-2{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(1-2{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3}=4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2+{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2+{x}_{3} \\\\ 1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $T(x)=b.$\n\nFinally, $2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2\\cdot 2+1=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124519 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a08e0a4-22c3-5139-848d-6f1027a61056" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Is in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32e9b3c3-cbf2-5bea-92ea-fb9d696cfc28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269734} {:answer/id #uuid "652a3f8b-3e85-546b-a3aa-07dda2d5208e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270170} {:answer/id #uuid "6dce70cc-ab59-5571-95a3-47994077e9fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270245} {:answer/id #uuid "7776181b-23ac-5afc-b4b2-52bf1b3e5e96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270327} {:answer/id #uuid "f09e9d3c-3983-5ca5-a168-c7d0bfaf254b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "652a3f8b-3e85-546b-a3aa-07dda2d5208e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270170} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e5c9936-6814-57f2-bf5a-ed4d9e501964" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7679a9d1-7c16-5065-b275-88941dd34726" :question/math-academy-id "q-140075" :question/problem "The linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{4}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}-{x}_{3},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}-{x}_{4} \\\\ -{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} \\\\ -11{x}_{3}-2{x}_{4}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b]$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-1){R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the false statement $0=-6.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that  $b\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124520 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e1baaf3c-f907-59dc-b9f9-a476a2889e73" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rank of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83d66d8c-c095-55c5-82e5-156fb6a77fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363177} {:answer/id #uuid "69b89807-97fa-57a7-88d2-88b07ce8c2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363178} {:answer/id #uuid "52b9287f-df57-59c5-b415-72f23606c6be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363179} {:answer/id #uuid "0c35c334-f400-5dc1-bf8d-276f2e3f2b88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363180} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c82834-ccb7-5845-b945-d22abbba57eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83d66d8c-c095-55c5-82e5-156fb6a77fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363177} :answer-field/id #uuid "d53a1aa4-cef0-536f-a0a5-781c11e47a1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "337abe4f-5c56-58ff-ad12-408abbd32afc" :question/math-academy-id "q-140078" :question/problem "Given the transformation\n $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 4z-2x \\\\ z-4x-3y\\end{aligned}],$ find its rank." :question/worked-solution "First, the standard matrix of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(T))=\\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $3$ pivot columns, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124519 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a08e0a4-22c3-5139-848d-6f1027a61056" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Is in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e68c41-7d11-5667-be88-8b3f622f7f5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364127} {:answer/id #uuid "e6138bdc-ad92-5fb3-9c42-e886a9d11925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364128} {:answer/id #uuid "984c4f5e-cccf-5dd1-99fa-0f9e667d083b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364129} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac5aac8-d2ab-5f03-ab3f-ee98c88f5e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364130} {:answer/id #uuid "86fc6401-f0c7-595e-8a1b-f6b905801c6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6138bdc-ad92-5fb3-9c42-e886a9d11925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364128} :answer-field/id #uuid "59e5ee21-2892-5089-bfdc-97a15df07f35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd6e26e7-9607-534b-af98-be903768ffa2" :question/math-academy-id "q-37936" :question/problem "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{3}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b],$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & 12\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Hence ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=-9\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-3-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-1-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1-{x}_{3} \\\\ -3-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2$, we have\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $T(x)=b.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=-3-(-5)=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124520 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e1baaf3c-f907-59dc-b9f9-a476a2889e73" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rank of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cdd4556-f390-586e-a185-92929f4605ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321882} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab7d6fa-64e4-52f9-b666-ac86edd24524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321980} {:answer/id #uuid "bea4854d-700e-5673-a945-c8cb48c2d381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322631} {:answer/id #uuid "d09cc216-abe8-55d3-bfa9-388d28127006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322775} {:answer/id #uuid "eebaffb5-3fb2-586c-93c3-08240b4b4830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cdd4556-f390-586e-a185-92929f4605ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321882} :answer-field/id #uuid "15b3ad90-3f42-5527-984a-20dea3819f0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76327d4f-74f6-5085-9904-77ad4c35f5a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-37937" :question/problem "If $T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -14 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(T))=\\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -14 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -14 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{14}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $2$ pivot columns, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124520 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e1baaf3c-f907-59dc-b9f9-a476a2889e73" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rank of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00710be5-863d-5c17-bb53-bf8bab61c96c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321151} {:answer/id #uuid "167da322-1e92-5a3f-b831-9c1ef41e52a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321331} {:answer/id #uuid "2da8e193-224c-503f-b046-f857038eefb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321502} {:answer/id #uuid "e05c361d-f1c3-5dd1-9f56-6ff974c22595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322901} {:answer/id #uuid "f9fae509-cd65-509b-acc8-b7b20d82154b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "167da322-1e92-5a3f-b831-9c1ef41e52a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321331} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8073d15-c3b1-5f9f-a153-35ee3466a57d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf501bcf-1b6e-5d01-99b4-4fd3685ab11a" :question/math-academy-id "q-38006" :question/problem "Given the transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}w \\\\ x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}w-x+2y-z \\\\ x-y-z \\\\ 4w-y+z \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ find its rank." :question/worked-solution "First, the standard matrix of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(T))=\\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -9 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-4{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $3$ pivot columns, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124519 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a08e0a4-22c3-5139-848d-6f1027a61056" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Is in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f36e9cc-aacc-5192-9693-90df97d5095f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395887} {:answer/id #uuid "fad570d2-bffe-52f4-9e6f-1b6feb83030c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395888} {:answer/id #uuid "1fd1ef26-855c-566b-b9a7-5c052f7787bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395889} {:answer/id #uuid "aeede097-4be9-58a3-86d7-462ab2d71c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395890} {:answer/id #uuid "189ac4b0-ba2c-501e-8f95-bc84de29cfe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fad570d2-bffe-52f4-9e6f-1b6feb83030c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395888} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea905565-2ed7-50de-9d3c-01672c6f67a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4809e77f-c615-585e-bbe9-4304896c33b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-38012" :question/problem "The linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{4}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{4},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{4}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ 5{x}_{2}-7{x}_{4}-3{x}_{1}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{2}+{x}_{4}+5{x}_{3}+2{x}_{1} \\\\ -7{x}_{3}-{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-{x}_{4}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -7 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b]$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 & -7 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5 & 1 & -9 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -7 & -1 & 10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & -3 & -15 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -5 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -14 & 0 & -18 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the foruth equation, we get the false statement $0=14.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that  $b\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124547 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4230d6ad-86c4-565e-8c5f-ab08179fc26f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Component of a Vector in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06bcdf92-07b0-569c-9165-d77ba0df6c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454921} {:answer/id #uuid "5025650c-e5a1-5ace-99b4-7b99f95165eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455046} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba74871-f43e-54b1-91de-5fca1601adfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455068} {:answer/id #uuid "dbfffd44-a1c8-5a29-9457-d499c05d41d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455282} {:answer/id #uuid "ff236ef4-985f-5272-aefc-da256a2ab1e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff236ef4-985f-5272-aefc-da256a2ab1e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455339} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d029871-7399-55eb-9f4e-0321150f3830" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6964e08a-7b43-5dcf-b2e7-f5084c3a12db" :question/math-academy-id "q-38612" :question/problem "Let $T=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$  such that $T(u)=v.$\n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot u & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4x-2y \\\\ 2x-8y \\\\ 6x+10y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-2y=2 \\\\ 2x-8y=8 \\\\ 6x+10y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=4+4y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2y & =2 \\\\ 4(4+4y)-2y & =2 \\\\ 14y & =-14 \\\\ y & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4+4y \\\\  & =4+4(-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-1$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+10y & =k \\\\ 6(0)+10(-1) & =k \\\\ k & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=-10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124547 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4230d6ad-86c4-565e-8c5f-ab08179fc26f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Component of a Vector in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ff62de4-85a2-5c35-aaf4-9b765d05dd69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324774} {:answer/id #uuid "6183d2d1-9a6e-519b-90ed-7e04299a8044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324785} {:answer/id #uuid "8c805c44-90a4-5f57-8f03-50f9d3dbc059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325164} {:answer/id #uuid "8f3d74ff-8b9e-5837-9590-93c8742df1d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325185} {:answer/id #uuid "e774b727-e979-5a78-8a5a-4bda8a6fc539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6183d2d1-9a6e-519b-90ed-7e04299a8044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324785} :answer-field/id #uuid "40f3498a-1822-53f0-a641-947f1e00c044" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aff78f44-088e-5944-87f1-2279a4c5e2ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-38615" :question/problem "Let $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-2x+y \\\\ -x+4y \\\\ -3x-5y\\end{aligned}]$ be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$ \n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2x+y \\\\ -x+4y \\\\ -3x-5y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+y=6 \\\\ -x+4y=-4 \\\\ -3x-5y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=4+4y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =6 \\\\ -2(4+4y)+y & =6 \\\\ -7y & =14 \\\\ y & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4+4y \\\\  & =4+4(-2) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-4$ and $y=-2$ into the third equation and obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x-5y & =k \\\\ -3(-4)-5(-2) & =k \\\\ k & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=22.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124520 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e1baaf3c-f907-59dc-b9f9-a476a2889e73" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rank of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f2d1dce-2cdb-5cd0-9945-a5894fae2637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455007} {:answer/id #uuid "4eff39a3-b584-51f5-96a7-86533868855d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455043} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad272aa-b7d5-5e56-8436-f1e116191ad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455064} {:answer/id #uuid "c4abd4a9-49c8-521f-a2a4-13af40b91fb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455242} {:answer/id #uuid "e640a410-dced-5b3f-bd71-c2ddadbb67db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e640a410-dced-5b3f-bd71-c2ddadbb67db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455303} :answer-field/id #uuid "29ff0008-ddaa-5c7f-ad04-1c919092c097" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ebab4da7-6448-59a5-b164-1d32d42c04ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-38620" :question/problem "Given the transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}w \\\\ x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}w-x+2y-z \\\\ x-y-z \\\\ 2w+x-3y+2z \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ find its rank." :question/worked-solution "First, the standard matrix of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(T))=\\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $3$ pivot columns, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124547 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4230d6ad-86c4-565e-8c5f-ab08179fc26f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Component of a Vector in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bcf98a3-43f3-5c33-abdd-b8d026c7a6b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324454} {:answer/id #uuid "618a014c-5312-57e8-bd68-f7ee954e2a04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324787} {:answer/id #uuid "9340202e-4ca3-5cc2-b068-0845c2860148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325224} {:answer/id #uuid "95446b96-c998-557d-8ce3-ac7cac93cbfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325242} {:answer/id #uuid "fa3f5d31-efe1-5f06-9d30-c4964f5a7779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa3f5d31-efe1-5f06-9d30-c4964f5a7779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326171} :answer-field/id #uuid "08724dfc-fcd7-5691-ba3b-59bd444d87b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5471fadb-1b86-5cb7-bba6-ebb075794d52" :question/math-academy-id "q-38627" :question/problem "Let $T=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$\n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot u & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3x-8y \\\\ 2x-4y \\\\ -2x+10y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-8y=6 \\\\ 2x-4y=8 \\\\ -2x+10y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=4+2y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-8y & =6 \\\\ 3(4+2y)-8y & =6 \\\\ -2y & =-6 \\\\ y & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4+2y \\\\  & =4+2(3) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=10$ and $y=3$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+10y & =k \\\\ -2(10)+10(3) & =k \\\\ k & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124519 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a08e0a4-22c3-5139-848d-6f1027a61056" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Is in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a866058-e522-5f43-b8d1-94192b2da129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421438} {:answer/id #uuid "905669cb-68f2-5e43-b764-abb5dd468fd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421439} {:answer/id #uuid "a39cfe50-b3ce-51ec-ab4c-7ce8f1bdf7fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421440} {:answer/id #uuid "a99db038-81b3-5562-9818-94b26695169a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421441} {:answer/id #uuid "adc76ae1-1330-5825-8435-4ad0e38e48d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "905669cb-68f2-5e43-b764-abb5dd468fd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421439} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e5a8161-1b5e-5e2b-bdbc-8100f0ce6ace" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dca0d5db-2f8d-5d5d-b6ae-e11ba42097a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-40209" :question/problem "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{3}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b],$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -4 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{2}{7}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.\nNow, from the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n7{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+3({x}_{3})-2{x}_{3}=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1$, we have\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $T(x)=b.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=1+1=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125150 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124520 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e1baaf3c-f907-59dc-b9f9-a476a2889e73" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rank of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95ab9d90-4c34-5b9d-b105-16cc8abae8c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325247} {:answer/id #uuid "986d4244-311f-59bd-a646-08d044649164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325266} {:answer/id #uuid "9e3b9c4a-3336-5e9e-bbf2-283739cb2e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325311} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f883ed-4d57-50b6-8f00-1b91a86881b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325345} {:answer/id #uuid "d42b3eb0-e3ac-52ab-adad-c6924982677e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e3b9c4a-3336-5e9e-bbf2-283739cb2e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325311} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3529463-3bcc-58dc-9826-6090e3740721" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f485de0-d115-59d2-87d8-fc0e53ab516c" :question/math-academy-id "q-40210" :question/problem "Given the transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+2y \\\\ y-x+6z \\\\ 8z-x+3y\\end{aligned}],$ find its rank." :question/worked-solution "First, the standard matrix of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(T))=\\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{5}{3}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $3$ pivot columns, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124547 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4230d6ad-86c4-565e-8c5f-ab08179fc26f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Component of a Vector in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04c01e0e-618d-5506-b9d5-fce990196878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348488} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea7cc94-7b3f-55b1-b387-efa1acd2cad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348860} {:answer/id #uuid "60ea3c46-d03c-599f-aefe-c4dcaa30279f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349331} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa2d6ad-abbc-5bd5-92e2-ac3a41f153a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349471} {:answer/id #uuid "f03a414b-4081-5887-999e-198f1ba28e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ea7cc94-7b3f-55b1-b387-efa1acd2cad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348860} :answer-field/id #uuid "00587c93-b1d8-5d2c-be26-6d8425903b89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ed740eb-f508-5f05-8f3f-7a98f936b4b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-80818" :question/problem "Let $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3x+y \\\\ x+2y \\\\ -2x+7y\\end{aligned}]$ be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$ \n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3x+y \\\\ x+2y \\\\ -2x+7y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+y=9 \\\\ x+2y=8 \\\\ -2x+7y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=8-2y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y & =9 \\\\ 3(8-2y)+y & =9 \\\\ -5y & =-15 \\\\ y & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =8-2y \\\\  & =8-2(3) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=2$ and $y=3$ into the third equation and obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+7y & =k \\\\ -2(2)+7(3) & =k \\\\ k & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=17.$"}]}
